# <%= title %>

<%= description %>

A <%= starter.name %> starter for [<%= theme.name %>](https://github.com/<%= theme.repo %>) <%= theme.version %>. Pages are plain GitHub Markdown; the theme's layouts, includes and CSS style them.

Live demo: <<%= starter.demo_url %>>

## Start

1. Create a repository from this template, or copy these files into an empty repository.
2. In **Settings > Pages**, set **Source** to **GitHub Actions**. `.github/workflows/pages.yml` builds the site, runs `modootoday-check` on it and deploys it on every push to `main`; pull requests are built and checked only.
3. Set `title` and `description` in `_config.yml`, change the header links in `_data/nav/main.yml` and the footer columns and copyright line in `_data/nav/footer.yml`, then replace the sample pages with yours.

## The live demo

The demo is this repository's own Pages site. Its build merges `_config.demo.yml` into `_config.yml`, which adds a showcase <%= starter.style %> linking to the theme's documentation and marks every page `noindex`. The workflow does this only when the repository is `<%= starter.repo %>`, so a repository created from this template, or a fork, builds the site without it. You can delete `_config.demo.yml` and the workflow's two demo steps.

## Stack

<% if remote? -%>
The theme loads with `remote_theme: <%= theme.repo %>@v<%= theme.version %>`, and the workflow builds the site with the same builder image as classic GitHub Pages, so no plugin outside the GitHub Pages list runs. To upgrade, change the tag in `_config.yml` and the version in `Gemfile`.
<% else -%>
Jekyll 4 with the theme gem, pinned in `Gemfile`. To upgrade, change its version there and run `bundle update <%= theme.name %>`.
<% end -%>

## Run it locally

```sh
bundle install
bundle exec jekyll serve
```
<% if remote? -%>

`jekyll serve` downloads the theme from GitHub (remote_theme), so it needs a network connection. The `Gemfile` also installs the theme gem for its two commands.
<% end -%>

## Add or rename a page

Every page has a Markdown twin at `<page>/index.md`. GitHub Pages runs no plugin that writes it, so each page has a small stub file beside it. After you add, rename or delete a page, write the stubs and check the site:

```sh
bundle exec modootoday-stubs . <%= stub_flags %> --write
bundle exec jekyll build
bundle exec modootoday-check . _site <%= check_flags %>
```
<% if starter.languages.size > 1 -%>

## Languages

Pages in the default language (`<%= starter.languages.first %>`) sit at the root. Translations live in one folder per language with the same page names: <%= starter.languages.drop(1).map { |l| "`#{l}/`" }.join(", ") %>. `_data/i18n/<language>.yml` overrides the theme's interface strings and `_data/nav/main-<language>.yml` is that language's menu.
<% end -%>

